<footer class="border-hairline border-t">
  {/*
   * Two columns, split by direction rather than by subject.
   *
   * Everything the site says about itself stays in one stack on the left: what
   * it is, who made it, whose the data is, where it was built and what it
   * promises. The right holds the one line that asks for something back, which
   * is also the only thing in the footer a visitor can act on. Alone in the
   * empty half it reads as an invitation; fourth in a list of statements it
   * read as another statement.
   *
   * One column below `md`, where two would be two narrow measures rather than
   * two groups. The left block still comes first, so the reading order is the
   * same either way.
   *
   * London sits in the sentence that says it rather than beside the year, so
   * nothing is stated twice.
   */}
  {/*
   * `data-nosnippet` is the direct fix for a thing that went wrong: Google
   * published the home page with this footer as its snippet, the wordmark and
   * the year and "I made the tool, not the data" and "handcrafted in London".
   * A snippet is built from prose, and the footer was the only prose in that
   * document — the rest of the home page is a headline, a field and three
   * numbers. The attribute is right on every page rather than only that one:
   * these five sentences are the same on half a million pages, so they can
   * never be the answer to what one of them is about.
   *
   * It sits on this div rather than on the `<footer>` because Google names
   * span, div and section as the elements it reads the attribute on, and a
   * sectioning element it does not name is not the place to find out. This div
   * holds every word in the footer, so the coverage is the same.
   *
   * A snippet rule only. The text is still indexed, the links are still
   * followed, and nothing changes for a reader.
   */}
  <div
    data-nosnippet
    class="column text-ink-faint grid gap-x-10 gap-y-6 py-11 font-mono text-xs leading-[1.5] tracking-wider md:grid-cols-2"
  >
    <div class="space-y-1">
      {/*
       * The one line that names the thing, so it is set apart from the five
       * that describe it. `font-semibold` is 600, which is a weight actually
       * downloaded rather than one the browser would have to fake, and it is
       * the same weight the wordmark carries in the header.
       */}
      <p class="font-semibold uppercase">dub.digger — the dub techno index</p>
      <p>
        MMXXVI <a href="https://simoneferraro.co.uk" rel="noreferrer" class="link-rule"
          >Simone Ferraro</a
        >
      </p>
      <p>I made the tool, not the data. That comes from Discogs.</p>
      <p>Website handcrafted in London. No tracking, no cookies, no ads, no third parties.</p>
      {/*
       * The claim is the link to the page that backs it. It was a flat sentence
       * for a while, which is a promise with nowhere to check it.
       */}
      <p>
        This website is designed to meet <a href="/accessibility" class="link-rule">WCAG 2.1 AA</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/*
     * Pushed to the far edge rather than left sitting at the halfway mark,
     * which lined up with nothing. Flush right it has the column's own boundary
     * to sit against, and the gap between the two blocks becomes the width of
     * the footer instead of an arbitrary indent.
     *
     * `text-right` as well as `justify-self-end`, so the second line stays
     * flush when the sentence wraps at the narrow end of `md`. Both are `md:`
     * only: below that the grid is one column and the whole footer reads left.
     */}
    <div class="md:justify-self-end md:text-right">
      <p>
        Questions, corrections, arguments:{" "}
        {/* Kept on one line: a control broken across two reads as two. */}
        <a
          href="mailto:simone.ferraro@nutek.it"
          data-contact-open
          class="link-rule whitespace-nowrap">Let's talk</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</footer>
